      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that all four math majors sit consecutively when nine people sit down at random seats around a round table, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people in a circular permutation.**\n   The number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people in a circular permutation is \\((n-1)!\\). For 9 people, this is:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Treat the group of 4 math majors as a single \"block.\"**\n   This reduces the problem to arranging 6 \"blocks\" (the math majors' block and the other 5 individuals) in a circle. The number of ways to arrange 6 blocks in a circle is \\((6-1)! = 5!\\):\n   \\[\n   5! = 120\n   \\]\n   Within the math majors' block, the 4 math majors can be arranged among themselves in:\n   \\[\n   4! = 24\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the total number of favorable arrangements is:\n   \\[\n   5! \\times 4! = 120 \\times 24 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability.**\n   The probability that all four math majors sit consecutively is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
